 ### [On-the-Fly Dynamic NeRF Training](7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md)
-On-the-fly dynamic NeRF training is a method for reconstructing dynamic scenes by training a neural radiance field concurrently with the acquisition of training frames, rather than after the event concludes [7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md]. Unlike existing dynamic NeRFs that require full video sequences to reconstruct 4D space-time, this approach enables streaming of the scene while it happens, requiring minimum optimization iterations per frame [7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Neural Transient Field (NeTF)](4692-neural-transient-field-netf.md) and [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) by treating the radiance field evolution as a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) process, optimizing only the current state based on the previous state and current observations, thereby ensuring scalability as time progresses [7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md].
-
-### Projected Color-Guided Dynamic NeRF
-A dynamic NeRF training method that uses projected color statistics (mean and variance) of sampled points to guide optimization via implicit point correspondence, replacing temporal deformation models [S1]. For synthetic datasets, it removes temporal components (temporal deformation model and temporal information enhancement) from the base TiNeuVox model and concatenates the mean and variance of the projected colors of the sampled point with its spatial feature as the input to the NeRF MLP [S1]. It replaces the multi-scale voxel grid used in TiNeuVox with the hash voxel grid used in InstantNGP, implemented with tiny-cuda-nn [S1]. For real-world datasets, it concatenates the mean and variance of the projected colors of the sampled point with its spatial feature queried from the hash voxel grid before inputting into the NeRF MLP [S1]. A warm-up duration for the first frame in synthetic datasets is used to compensate for slower convergence of hashed voxels [S1]. Specular surfaces and occluded points violate the relative invariance of projected color, causing artifacts [S1]. It is implemented on top of TiNeuVox for synthetic datasets and InstantNGP (via NerfAcc) for real-world datasets [S1].
+On-the-fly dynamic NeRF training is a method for reconstructing dynamic scenes by optimizing the radiance field concurrently with the acquisition of training frames, enabling real-time streaming of the event as it unfolds. Unlike standard dynamic NeRFs that require full video sequences for offline 4D reconstruction, this approach minimizes optimization iterations per frame to achieve interactive speeds. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Neural Transient Field (NeTF)](4692-neural-transient-field-netf.md) and [Projected Color-Guided Dynamic NeRF](7523-projected-color-guided-dynamic-nerf.md) by explicitly formulating the training process as a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) estimation problem, leveraging a first-order Markov assumption to maintain scalability over time.
+#### D-NeRF
+**Definition**: A method that treats time $t$ as an additional input for system rendering [S1].
+**Step**: Controls both camera view and time variables for dynamic rendering [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of on-the-fly dynamic NeRF training was introduced in "OD-NeRF: Efficient Training of On-the-Fly Dynamic Neural Radiance Fields" [S1]. Prior dynamic NeRFs focused on reconstructing scenes given full training videos of a dynamic event, often using space-time 4D representations or explicit deformation fields [S1]. These models with temporal inputs often extrapolate poorly to unseen time and require many iterations of optimization for each frame [S1]. OD-NeRF addressed the challenge of efficient on-the-fly training by proposing a NeRF model conditioned on multi-view projected colors to implicitly track correspondence between current and previous frames, and a transition and update algorithm that leverages the occupancy grid from the last frame to sample efficiently at the current frame [S1]. The method achieves an interactive speed of 6 FPS training and rendering on synthetic dynamic scenes on-the-fly [S1].
+Dynamic NeRF methods emerged to address the limitations of static reconstruction in non-rigid scenes. Early approaches, such as D-NeRF, introduced time as an explicit input to the neural network, allowing the model to learn temporal variations in appearance and geometry [S1]. Subsequent work explored deformation fields to map dynamic observations to a canonical space, and more recent methods have focused on real-time streaming and handling complex temporal dependencies through probabilistic models like Hidden Markov Models [S1].
 
